WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.410
I'm going to be speaking with Robert Fatalis,
who is professor of political science at the

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.889
University of Pennsylvania and also author
of the book Oil Craft The Myths of Scarcity

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:15.049
and Security That Haunt US Energy Policy.

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:16.049
So great to have you on.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:20.130
I think at the time of a presidential transition
where there are questions about if and how

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.199
policy will change around oil, it's a it's
a very timely conversation.

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:25.199
Thanks.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:26.490
It's great to be here.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:34.300
So I guess to start with, when you talk about
oil craft or the trick that is sometimes played

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.170
with the topic of oil, like what is the big
trick?

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.290
What's the big misunderstanding?

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:43.859
Well, the big misunderstanding is that you
don't have to do anything special to guarantee

00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:49.780
access to it, especially during peacetime,
meaning there's a world market in oil.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:50.809
One just buys it.

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:52.890
That is how the military gets its oil.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.410
That is how all domestic refiners get their
oil.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:04.750
So all talk about need to use military power
to secure access or against threats are pretty

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.610
much untrue.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.620
And can you talk a little bit about why?

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.530
So there's there's probably some people who
will hear you say that and they'll say, OK,

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.330
well, there's an aspect of that that makes
intuitive sense.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:22.820
But at the same time, the world wouldn't necessarily
grind to a halt if access to a different commodity

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.490
like pork bellies were somehow impacted.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:30.780
It seems as though because so much transportation
depends on oil, so many products, including

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.200
products that have plastic, come from oil
and so many different things, it seems that

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.060
the implications are different.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.990
Even if on paper we're talking about two commodities,
why do you argue that there is not really

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.480
a difference?

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:51.320
Because the vision of access somehow being
denied by some crisis is also something that

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.140
hasn't existed in in our lives.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:02:00.150
So, for instance, right now, access is being
hindered in all sorts of way to oil supplies,

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.970
but we hardly think about it.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.620
What is the perfect example of that?

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:10.289
The United States government has sanctions
on various governments that are oil producers.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:18.969
Venezuela on the one hand, Iran on the other,
that hinders access in in real ways.

00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:20.659
But we don't talk about it.

00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:26.209
Instead, what we imagine is some kind of,
I don't know, like a disaster movie where

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:30.280
all oil, all oil production collapses.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.569
We've never had an experience like that.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:37.239
But we we generate constant, scary scenarios.

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:44.989
One of the global relationships that uses
oil as a justification for being sort of a

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:50.189
special relationship or one that needs to
be treated differently is the U.S. relationship

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:51.849
with Saudi Arabia.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:56.980
And sometimes if you step back, it might be
sort of confusing, given the democratic values

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.489
that so many administrations have claimed
to want to spread around the world and in

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:04.620
fact, many of the military conflicts based
on spreading those ideas.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.909
It would seem counterintuitive that we would
have the relationship we do with Saudi Arabia,

00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:13.560
which represents much of what we seek to change
in that region and elsewhere in the world.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:20.249
But oil and a shared goal of stability in
the region is often cited as the explanation.

00:03:20.249 --> 00:03:26.980
How does your perspective on oil modify how
maybe we should be thinking of that relationship?

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.940
Well, one that is correct.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:35.739
Oil is used as the explanation for what we
ostensibly otherwise would not do.

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:36.739
Right.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:43.749
So and, you know, basically that's another
scary scenario to that has that have trip

00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:46.040
that has tripped people in the past.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:51.939
So Americans who otherwise would not think
we have some kind of we should be so closely

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:56.749
involved with Saudi Arabia except OK, but
we need their oil.

00:03:56.749 --> 00:04:04.079
And and so we have to kind of swallow our
pride or not pursue our more idealistic side.

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:10.930
And that's fundamentally incorrect, as my
favorite oil economist of the 1970s said back

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:17.100
then, and it remains true until now, the one
thing Saudi Arabia has to do is sell their

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.640
oil on the world market.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.490
The one thing we don't have to worry about
is access to oil from Saudi Arabia or anyone

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.730
else has has to worry about.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.680
Are there other relationships.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.840
So you mentioned Venezuela.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.570
We know about the relationship with Saudi
Arabia.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:40.250
Are there other geopolitical alliances or
relationships that that are important when

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.660
it comes to oil that are much less discussed
in media and that people may not understand

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.660
the role of?

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.720
Well, I think, folks, you can just think about
the US position in the Persian Gulf, generally,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.280
not just in Saudi Arabia, but in.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:05:01.040
Bahrain, where the Fifth Fleet is located
in Qatar, in the Emirates and and so forth,

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.950
we have this understanding that we have to
be in the Persian Gulf to protect access to

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:06.950
oil.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:10.940
And what I've argued and what the military
has said time and time again in other experts

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.190
is that that is incorrect.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:20.150
And so the real question should be, so what's
behind that, behind the rationale of strategic

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.750
access to oil?

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:29.500
What do these regimes actually do for us that
that keep us so closely tied to them?

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:35.100
I want to get to eventually in our conversation,
some of the reasons why we've not moved off

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.300
of oil more quickly.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:42.580
But before addressing that, because there
are so many geopolitical relationships that

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:48.030
are connected to oil, there are significant,
it would seem, diplomatic and foreign policy

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:54.860
implications that would that would rise if
indeed the U.S. and the world were to accelerate

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:00.850
getting off of oil and towards renewable energy
and other types of energy sources.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:06.980
Is that intuition accurate in a sense, or
do you believe that there would simply be

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:12.070
new justifications given for maintaining the
status quo, which now oil is pointed to as

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.260
the explanation?

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.850
I'm you know, I'm usually trust professors
to predict anything.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:25.290
But of course, that said, I lean toward your
second option there.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.290
Right.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:34.440
Other rationales will be created to continue
to pursue the policies that we pursue now

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:40.470
that whatever else they do have no impact
on, on access to oil.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:41.891
We have a perfect example of this.

00:06:41.891 --> 00:06:47.140
The United States is has has a large military
presence in the Persian Gulf.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:55.420
In the past weeks, Saudi oil facilities or
oil transport have been bombed by ships.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:07:03.290
And and the US is unable to to prevent the
precise kinds of threats that we often think

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:06.650
is at the heart of our military presence there.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:10.560
But does the the military want to stay there?

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:11.650
Absolutely.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:18.070
All else equal the military presence is a
benefit to the military.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:25.180
They've never been really behind any kind
of a retreat from positions where they already

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:30.890
secured what would be the and again, maybe
I'm asking for a prediction or maybe maybe

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:38.480
we have a reason to think one way or the other
if the excuse of oil were no longer credible

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:43.340
in terms of explaining a U.S. Saudi relationship,
that doesn't seem to make much sense for the

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.470
reasons we discussed earlier.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:50.230
What do you imagine would replace it as the
justification will eat?

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:56.021
Well, whether it would work or not, whether
Americans would come to say, OK, yes, we have

00:07:56.021 --> 00:08:00.940
to do it, we have to accept our ties to the
Saudis.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.690
We have no other choice.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:04.970
Well, one is a new one.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:12.000
This is the one credit I give to Mohammed
bin Salman, the de facto ruler, which is that

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:19.430
also to be a it's a it's a it's a plus on
the side of Israel.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:24.460
And those Americans who really think it's
important that the Saudis are part of a coalition

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:26.460
with Israel against Iran.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:27.460
Right.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:32.060
And you notice in that discussion there's
no talk about oil or the oil disappears.

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:33.060
Right.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:40.620
And in fact, we'd be better off if Iran was
producing its oil unhindered by the United

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:41.620
States.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.920
It's of course, it's, of course, producing
oil, but it's doing it with the assistance

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.819
of China at this point.

00:08:46.819 --> 00:08:52.360
OK, so when we think about why we've not moved
away from oil more quickly, we seem to have

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:57.240
the technological know how to do it to a greater
degree than it has has been done.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.999
And there are all sorts of explanations that
are often given.

00:08:59.999 --> 00:09:04.529
Some are more foreign policy related, including
what you and I have been talking about.

00:09:04.529 --> 00:09:09.959
Some claim that we simply don't have the efficiency
in some of these alternatives to oil to generate

00:09:09.959 --> 00:09:14.670
the energy we need, that while solar panels
are improving and wind is improving, we're

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:15.810
simply not there yet.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.310
And there's a debate about that.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:22.240
And it seems that to a great extent it's not
true is part about political willingness to

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.240
do it.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.930
But but maybe to some degree we're not quite
there.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:27.470
There are other explanations given as well
in terms of will.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.870
It would damage this industry well, but it
would prop this one up.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:35.720
And then you have to kind of balance whether
it would be a net job creator or destroyer.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:39.370
If you had to give us a general assessment
of why we've not moved away from oil more

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.259
quickly, what would it include?

00:09:41.259 --> 00:09:43.160
A political will?

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:53.399
I think the obfuscating arguments of multiple,
you know, vested interests in this process.

00:09:53.399 --> 00:09:59.610
I've watched this, I've studied how these
debates took place in the 1970s during the

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:06.230
so-called energy crisis, and there was a lot
of confusion out there because to people of

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:10.910
different interests tend to argue their positions.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:16.589
They tend to exaggerate, you know, the the
significance or the or the or the importance

00:10:16.589 --> 00:10:20.350
or the security threats to their various positions.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:22.120
So I think lack of political will.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.990
Yes, the technology is not completely there
yet.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:31.430
But at the same time, I've noticed something
are happening for the first time, which is

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:36.199
the oil industry itself, or at least the largest
segments of the oil industry.

00:10:36.199 --> 00:10:44.450
The global the global producers are arguing,
in fact, they are preparing for a peak, not

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:50.629
supply, which is how we used to talk about
it, but peak demand that in fact, their predictions

00:10:50.629 --> 00:10:57.490
are oil demand for oil will be declining over
the medium term, etc..

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.629
So I think something is happening.

00:10:59.629 --> 00:11:06.300
And what do they say would be the the catalysts
for those declines in demand?

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:13.930
Concerns about global warming, the environment
and the committee commitments on, I mean,

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:19.959
the commitments of various domestic groups
to the fears of what climate change mean,

00:11:19.959 --> 00:11:23.379
plus the plus the developments in the technology.

00:11:23.379 --> 00:11:25.710
That's what economists have said.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:32.819
At some point, oil will oil will simply become
a wasted asset that will remain in the ground

00:11:32.819 --> 00:11:39.779
because technology will shift such that we
can we we will have a better energy source

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:41.410
sources to rely on.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:46.249
So along those lines, the President Trump,
the one that still president for a few more

00:11:46.249 --> 00:11:52.269
days, he has said many times during his rallies
and during various briefings, even briefings

00:11:52.269 --> 00:11:56.720
unrelated to energy as they often are, he
has uttered this this phrase that we are now

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.760
energy independent.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.619
First question is, is that true?

00:12:02.619 --> 00:12:11.240
And then a second question is, has fracking
impacted the U.S. energy picture in a significant

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:18.009
enough way that it has separated us a little
bit from the geopolitical oil situation?

00:12:18.009 --> 00:12:20.509
OK, great questions.

00:12:20.509 --> 00:12:27.839
No, we are not energy independent because
we live in a one world market for oil.

00:12:27.839 --> 00:12:28.839
Right.

00:12:28.839 --> 00:12:31.360
We believe, you know, we have this fiction.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:37.600
We think that domestic sources of supply is
what the country first relies on.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.420
And then and only then does it does it a turn
to export?

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.869
That has never been true, number one.

00:12:44.869 --> 00:12:51.069
Number two, even to the extent that we are
now producing more oil through hydraulic fracturing

00:12:51.069 --> 00:12:57.670
than we had in the then than we've had for
a long time, the price of that oil depends

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.369
on events in the world market.

00:13:00.369 --> 00:13:09.459
So there is no way to stop the what drives
us most crazy in the day to day the volatility

00:13:09.459 --> 00:13:15.959
and price of oil right now where prices are
down.

00:13:15.959 --> 00:13:19.390
But we remember back when barrels of oil were
under two dollars.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:28.510
Well, in the future, we are not able to put
up walls against those price shocks unless

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:34.730
we turn to a kind of a domestic politics that
we haven't seen in this country for decades,

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:38.269
which was which would be to create a new protectionist
regime.

00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:39.269
Right.

00:13:39.269 --> 00:13:46.029
Not permit any or not permit any imports of
oil and rely completely on domestic production.

00:13:46.029 --> 00:13:48.939
Well, you know, that's been a failure in the
past.

00:13:48.939 --> 00:13:52.639
It's caused all sorts of problems in the United
States.

00:13:52.639 --> 00:13:56.190
And it's hard to imagine returning to that
now.

00:13:56.190 --> 00:14:01.040
Has has fracking reduced our dependency?

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.529
No, because it's it's just not the case.

00:14:04.529 --> 00:14:09.830
So there's one there's an easy way to see
that we can look at our oil import figures

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:12.880
and they're out.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:14.370
We still imported oil.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:22.449
Right, because certain users prefer the their
own refineries are geared toward certain kinds

00:14:22.449 --> 00:14:23.449
of oil.

00:14:23.449 --> 00:14:28.369
And it's very difficult for them to move away
from the oil that they tend to use.

00:14:28.369 --> 00:14:31.779
Let me give you one good example that people
never think of.

00:14:31.779 --> 00:14:39.110
Yeah, folks argued they expected that with
hydraulic fracturing, we would not any longer

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.660
be dependent on Saudi Arabia and Iran.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:44.029
Imports from Saudi Arabia would decline.

00:14:44.029 --> 00:14:45.300
Well, that's not true.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:52.660
Why people forget that Saudi Arabia is itself
a major refiner in the United States.

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:59.480
The largest amount of Saudi oil coming to
the United States is to is to supply their

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:05.639
own refineries in the United States in the
same way that a global oil company like Exxon

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:06.639
would do.

00:15:06.639 --> 00:15:11.629
So setting up refineries in various countries
in order to have guaranteed markets for their

00:15:11.629 --> 00:15:12.629
crude, etc..

00:15:12.629 --> 00:15:18.319
So Saudi Arabia continues to import Saudi
Arabian oil into Saudi refineries in the United

00:15:18.319 --> 00:15:19.839
States.

00:15:19.839 --> 00:15:24.810
Real quick, the last thing I wanted to touch
on and my my expectation is your commentary

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:29.350
on this would be more geopolitical than technological,
but I'm glad to hear your thoughts.

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:35.269
Either way, there are folks on the left who
are sometimes divided over the role of new

00:15:35.269 --> 00:15:44.610
nuclear capacity and technology as a possible
bridge from the current paradigm to renewables.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:50.040
And there are some who don't like the sort
of risk of nuclear.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.699
While the more nuclear engineers I talk to,
the more they say that those risks are really

00:15:54.699 --> 00:15:59.119
based on 40, 50 year old technology, which
is not what would be used if new nuclear was

00:15:59.119 --> 00:16:00.119
put online.

00:16:00.119 --> 00:16:04.890
So I'm sort of open to the idea of new nuclear
as a bridge, Mike.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:09.100
Concern is that practically, because it takes
a while to bring that capacity on, it seems

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:13.279
it would be difficult to commit to it merely
being a bridge.

00:16:13.279 --> 00:16:17.369
What is your sort of thought about new nuclear
as and its role?

00:16:17.369 --> 00:16:22.290
Well, I think your your vision of that is
yours is mine.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.920
It's I think is is the correct one.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:32.199
And also what doesn't often get talked about
is the fact that those new nuclear plants

00:16:32.199 --> 00:16:36.920
would be subsidized by the by government in
one form or another.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:37.920
Right.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.350
It's very hard to do it economically now without
subsidy.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:48.199
So you can imagine another new industry seeking
know, seeking those subsidies in order to

00:16:48.199 --> 00:16:52.929
in order to get new plants operational.

00:16:52.929 --> 00:16:59.110
You know, the text I read on this question,
the debate is still open.

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:03.700
I've seen I've seen books by actually old
friends of mine from graduate school have

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:10.140
argued this is this is the only real way forward,
especially if what we care about is generating

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:17.310
electricity for the millions of people in
India and elsewhere that still have no electricity.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:25.550
Renewables are not going to be able to to
to give the development that folks need around

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:26.550
the world.

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:27.760
And nuclear is the way to do that.

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:31.960
But I've also seen technologies challenge,
challenge that claim.

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:36.970
So I think what we're going to see is, I mean,
I can't resolve that right.

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:39.230
To see as more flights around that question.

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:46.690
And it's certainly there is certainly a visceral
argument against it on the part of the left.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:53.809
And I think that goes back to, you know, back
to the 70s, to Chernobyl, to Three Mile Island

00:17:53.809 --> 00:17:55.649
and so forth, to no nukes.

00:17:55.649 --> 00:17:57.650
There was there were the concerns back then.

00:17:57.650 --> 00:17:58.650
Right.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.029
And the left is with you on this.

00:18:01.029 --> 00:18:09.059
The left seems more or less committed to the
idea of green New Deal renewables supporting

00:18:09.059 --> 00:18:16.730
fossil fuels or any other kind of industry
at the same time.

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:17.730
Yep, I agree.

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:21.789
An issue of critical importance and one, maybe
two to expand on in the future.

00:18:21.789 --> 00:18:26.230
We've been speaking with Robert Fatalis, who's
a professor of political science at the University

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:31.850
of Pennsylvania, also author of the book Oil
Craft The Myths of Scarcity and Security That

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:33.490
Haunt US Energy Policy.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:36.610
Thanks so much for your time today and take
good care of The David Pakman Show at David

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:36.650
Pakman Dotcom.

